  "query_preview": "..A strong area of high pressure continues to dominate\nthe Southwest, and will continue to do so through Friday\nafternoon. This pattern will keep dry conditions and warm\ntemperatures around for the time being. The synoptic pattern will\nbegin to shift by mid-day Friday, as a couple of disturbances \npush in from off the West Coast. The first shortwave on Friday \nafternoon will take a southerly trajectory, keeping precipitation \nchances limited to areas along and south of the Mogollon Rim. A \nlarger trough following closely behind will move across northern \nArizona on Saturday. Our forecast has more scattered to widespread\ncoverage for showers throughout Saturday, but overall rainfall \naccumulation appear to be less than 0.25\". Snow levels also are \nforecast to remain above 8000', so only areas such as the Kaibab \nPlateau, San Fransisco Peaks, and the White Mountains are \nexpecting snow accumulation. Even so, snow accumulation doesn't\nlook overly impressive with these areas likely only seeing\n0.1-1.5\". Outside of precipitation, gusty southwest winds are also\nexpected on Saturday, especially during the early morning hours\nwhere we could see localized gusts near 30-40 mph. \n\nSunday and Monday...Another ridge will build over the Southwest,\nbringing warmer and drier conditions. \n\nTuesday...A colder and stronger trough looks to swing across much\nof the Western CONUS on Tuesday, bringing additional precipitation\nand cooler conditions. With this system being on the colder side,\nsnow levels look to drop closer to 7000-7500', so areas such as\nFlagstaff, Parks, Happy Jack, and Kachina Village could see a\nlight dusting. There is still time to hash out all the details of\nthis system, so check back later for more information.",
